---
import AppShell from '../../layouts/AppShell.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../../components/Breadcrumb.astro';
import { ORGANIZATION_REF, SOCIAL_IMAGE_URL, WEBSITE_REF } from '../../lib/seo';

export const prerender = true;

const title = 'AI Automation Consultant in NJ and NYC | Neil Busque';
const description = 'Neil Busque finds the processes costing your team time, then builds the automation to fix them. Direct, hands-on consulting in NJ, NYC, and remote.';
const canonical = 'https://busqueneil.com/services/ai-automation-consultant';

const service = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Service',
  '@id': `${canonical}#service`,
  name: 'AI Automation Consultant',
  url: canonical,
  image: SOCIAL_IMAGE_URL,
  provider: ORGANIZATION_REF,
  brand: ORGANIZATION_REF,
  isPartOf: WEBSITE_REF,
  mainEntityOfPage: { '@id': `${canonical}#webpage` },
  areaServed: ['New Jersey', 'New York', 'New York City', 'United States', 'Remote'],
  serviceType: ['AI automation consulting', 'Workflow automation', 'Business process automation', 'AI strategy'],
  description,
};
---
<AppShell title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} active="services">
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <Breadcrumb items={[
      { name: 'Home', url: 'https://busqueneil.com/' },
      { name: 'Services', url: 'https://busqueneil.com/services' },
      { name: 'AI Automation Consultant', url: canonical },
    ]} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(service)} />
  </Fragment>

  <header class="page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span class="idx">&#8727;</span> Services &#183; Consulting</p>
    <h1>AI automation <span class="accent">consultant</span>.</h1>
    <p class="lede">Most teams do not need more software. They need someone to trace where the work gets stuck, decide what should change, and build the fix.</p>
  </header>

  <article class="article-page">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What an AI automation consultant does</h2>
      <p>The work has two parts. First, trace the process closely enough to find the real bottleneck. Then build the fix and put it into the tools your team already uses. A strategy deck can be useful, but it does not run your follow-up on Tuesday morning.</p>
      <p>I handle both parts. The person asking the questions is also the person building the system.</p>

      <h2>When it is worth hiring one</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Your team spends hours on work that needs no judgment.</li>
        <li>Leads or tickets go cold because follow-up depends on someone remembering.</li>
        <li>You have bought tools that do not talk to each other, so a person is the integration.</li>
        <li>You know something should be automated but not what, or in what order.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If you already know exactly what needs building, skip the consulting framing entirely and just tell me what to build.</p>

      <h2>Consultant, agency, or in-house</h2>
      <p>An agency gives you a team and a repeatable process. An in-house hire gives you long-term ownership. A hands-on consultant makes sense when the problem is bounded, speed matters, and you want a direct line to the builder. If your problem really needs a permanent owner, I will say so on the first call.</p>

      <h2>How I run it</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Diagnosis call.</strong> Where the hours go, what breaks, what you have already tried.</li>
        <li><strong>Scope in writing.</strong> What gets automated, what does not, and what done looks like.</li>
        <li><strong>Build.</strong> Days, not quarters. You see it as it goes.</li>
        <li><strong>Handoff or keep me.</strong> It is your system either way.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>What I build with</h2>
      <p>n8n, Zapier, and GoHighLevel for workflows, and Claude with MCP tools where the task needs judgment. I use plain automation wherever a rule would do, because a model where a rule belongs is how automation becomes expensive and unpredictable. More on <a href="/services/ai-automation-services">AI automation services</a> and <a href="/services/ai-agent-development">AI agent development</a>.</p>

      <h2>Serving New Jersey, NYC, and remote</h2>
      <p>I am Neil Busque, based in Elizabeth, New Jersey. I work with teams across the NJ and NYC metro and remotely across the United States, in Eastern time. Most of the work happens remotely no matter where you are.</p>

      <h2>See how I work first</h2>
      <p>In 2026 I shipped more than 40 web apps, AI agents, and automations. Before that, I shipped over 20 production n8n workflows across more than 10 client accounts, including an onboarding system that cut a 30-day process to 7 days. See <a href="/work">the work</a>, read <a href="/resources">the guides</a>, then <a href="/contact">tell me where your hours go</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <div class="hire-cta v8-card">
    <span class="eyebrow">The guarantee</span>
    <p class="guarantee">I keep working until you are proud of it. If I am not the right fit, I will tell you on the first call.</p>
    <div class="hero-ctas">
      <a class="btn-sun" href="https://book.busqueneil.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Book a call</a>
      <a class="btn-ghost" href="https://wa.me/19083164140?text=Hi%20Neil%2C%20I%20have%20a%20project%20in%20mind" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Or message me on WhatsApp</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</AppShell>
